#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b835315-d2fd-5823-9fb6-0f5535d3d549" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the Central Limit Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d40bd2c-dcd9-59ac-b0e2-c7d52d41a210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1539" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483616} {:answer/id #uuid "1f98fc2b-1e1b-5ec3-bfa8-3c6f3b84ee29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1587" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483618} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0c2446-935e-58d5-bb0b-b9f993444007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1814" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483643} {:answer/id #uuid "91359df8-d289-5d02-8538-3a34ade27caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2061" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483868} {:answer/id #uuid "b80bbc72-56db-5e70-9204-c74bdefa7b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2119" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f98fc2b-1e1b-5ec3-bfa8-3c6f3b84ee29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1587" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483618} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd48dcdf-ec5c-553b-93df-1ad0ce12e474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2c4c6dc-06db-5c59-88cb-4cad1074fe93" :question/math-academy-id "q-143480" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{121}$ be a random sample of size $n=121$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =300$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =55.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $295.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=121,\\,\\mu =300,\\,\\sigma =55.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(300,\\frac{{55}^{2}}{121}) \\\\  & ∼N(300,25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<295).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<295) & =P(Z<\\frac{295-300}{\\sqrt{25}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-\\frac{5}{5}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1) \\\\  & =Φ(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1)=0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<295) & =Φ(-1) \\\\  & =0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327e406f-f432-5db4-a79f-80195c02cc96" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ba174f-6f4c-59fc-b3cb-137e1297aa50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5429" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483689} {:answer/id #uuid "554630d6-0001-5d34-98b7-ae518e793543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483721} {:answer/id #uuid "82de29c2-442b-50b3-b8a2-f2cef0536930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5678" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483833} {:answer/id #uuid "da0abab7-344b-5db3-b96e-606236a85502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6601" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484000} {:answer/id #uuid "e46f85da-0699-50b2-9c52-f46103534b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da0abab7-344b-5db3-b96e-606236a85502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6601" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484000} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa4e5556-e66f-5ee1-bddc-c98cca0920b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95a41c31-9a32-5733-b64d-135e1d1b45d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143502" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n=30$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66,$ calculate $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0606\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0594\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0582\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0735\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0721\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0708\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0885\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0869\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0853\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7088\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7123\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7157\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7422\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7454\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7486\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7734\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7764\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7794\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{2}{15}{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{45}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{128}{45})-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{45}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5} \\\\  & =2.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.66.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.2,\\frac{0.66}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.2,0.022).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =P(\\frac{2-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}<Z<\\frac{2.3-2.2}{\\sqrt{0.022}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.35<Z<0.67) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.67)-P(Z<-1.35) \\\\  & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.67)=0.7486, \\\\ Φ(-1.35)=0.0885.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<\\overset{―}{X}<2.3) & =Φ(0.67)-Φ(-1.35) \\\\  & =0.7486-0.0885 \\\\  & =0.6601.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "327e406f-f432-5db4-a79f-80195c02cc96" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Continuous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c5bf3b4-ed42-5eb0-ad5a-8ac0efb22629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0985" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061267} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d4ec58-1e94-5b26-987f-f90f2a1f6bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1170" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061268} {:answer/id #uuid "f23c9b14-6f45-5ea6-87c4-ad37be574c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061269} {:answer/id #uuid "030e07aa-4ecb-5bb4-bc80-5f5cbb2506a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0853" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061270} {:answer/id #uuid "090ff02b-aeb9-5a1d-b36c-5054afc4b296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c5bf3b4-ed42-5eb0-ad5a-8ac0efb22629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0985" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061267} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cc45ee8-b0e6-5e8f-9a87-c2295533ec34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6df49040-5dbb-5927-a6aa-8f742c1a6cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143503" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{25}$ be a random sample of size $n=25$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.15,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6).$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the sample size is sufficiently large for the central limit theorem to be applied. A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{32}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.15.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.5,\\frac{0.15}{25}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.5,0.006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6) & =P(Z>\\frac{1.6-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.006}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.29)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>1.29) & =1-P(Z<1.29) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.29).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.29)=0.9015.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6) & =1-Φ(1.29) \\\\  & =1-0.9015 \\\\  & =0.0985.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9722c125-2d26-5aed-ab60-2e2358a17fdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Discrete"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58e231ce-e2dc-5918-a3f7-c22726470c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1)-Φ(-1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483730} {:answer/id #uuid "900ec749-ed02-5243-a1b6-eea5f267a77c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2)-Φ(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483865} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fe1f0e-03f6-5c1b-9efa-37601b693f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2.5)-Φ(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483939} {:answer/id #uuid "c02cc025-e442-50e2-9860-12b5a1a52c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.5)-Φ(-1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483947} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf42980-f617-5f97-b30d-0955b76d372f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1)-Φ(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "900ec749-ed02-5243-a1b6-eea5f267a77c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2)-Φ(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483865} :answer-field/id #uuid "9247b964-7703-5bda-8b8b-02af0509ca08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5574158c-e378-5c22-867d-7f3ecb6e0b26" :question/math-academy-id "q-143513" :question/problem "| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{116}$ be a random sample of size $n=116$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $3$ and $5.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=116,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.6+5\\cdot 0.1+10\\cdot 0.2+15\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0+0.5+2+1.5 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{5}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{10}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{15}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+2.5+20+22.5)-16 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(4,\\frac{29}{116}) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5) & ≈P(\\frac{3-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}<Z<\\frac{5-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}) \\\\  & =P(-2<Z<2) \\\\  & =Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5)=Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064314 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9722c125-2d26-5aed-ab60-2e2358a17fdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the CLT With a Population Whose Probability Distribution Is Discrete"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0e9d16-85d3-5733-86dd-b370bb650371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483617} {:answer/id #uuid "2218abf4-e023-5af3-b0eb-30e08e6ee534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483627} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9ebc6d-26da-5439-8344-0e789ccaddc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483815} {:answer/id #uuid "c7238fb6-2845-5bcb-8fb3-76e79b105b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483960} {:answer/id #uuid "d30411eb-c7c7-50c0-be48-8d31e7a05041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d30411eb-c7c7-50c0-be48-8d31e7a05041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483990} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fbc75be-8e9b-55b7-b598-a4421edd592d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7a75f10-4331-5d53-8955-dd0d91fe7458" :question/math-academy-id "q-143514" :question/problem "| $x$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $-4$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{65}$ be a random sample of size $n=65$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $-4.9.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=65,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 0.1+(-6)\\cdot 0.4+(-4)\\cdot 0.4+(-2)\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-0.8-2.4-1.6-0.2 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-8)^{2}\\cdot 0.1+(-6)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-(-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(6.4+14.4+6.4+0.4)-25 \\\\  & =2.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(-5,\\frac{2.6}{65}) \\\\  & ∼N(-5,0.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9) & ≈P(Z<\\frac{-4.9-(-5)}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.5) \\\\  & =Φ(0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.9)=Φ(0.5).\n$$"}]}
